Consider the code below. The statement prints `A`.

```cpp
int x = 5;
if (x = 3)
    cout << "A";
else
    cout << "B";
```

Consider the code below. Evaluating the `if` condition attempts to compute `10 / x`, causing division by zero.

```cpp
int x = 0;
if (x != 0 && 10 / x > 2)
    cout << "A";
else
    cout << "B";
```

The following code prints exactly `BC`.

```cpp
int x = 2;
switch (x) {
    case 1: cout << "A";
    case 2: cout << "B";
    case 3: cout << "C";
    default: cout << "D";
}
```

The following program prints `C`.

```cpp
int x = 10, y = 2;
if (x > 5)
    if (y > 5)
        cout << "A";
    else
        cout << "B";
else
    cout << "C";
```

For the code below, `Valid` is printed even though x = 100 is not between 1 and 10.

```cpp
int x = 100;
if (1 < x < 10)
    cout << "Valid";
else
    cout << "Invalid";
```

The following program stores 2.5 in `result`.

```cpp
int x = 5, y = 2;
double result = (x > y) ? x / y : y / x;
```

After executing the following code, `x` has the value 6.

```cpp
int x = 5;
if (x == 5 || ++x > 5)
    cout << "A";
```

The following is a valid C++ `switch` statement and will print `A`.

```cpp
double x = 2.0;
switch (x) {
    case 2: cout << "A"; break;
    default: cout << "B";
}
```

The following code prints both `A` and `B` because both comparisons are true.

```cpp
int score = 95;
if (score >= 90)
    cout << "A";
else if (score >= 80)
    cout << "B";
else
    cout << "C";
```

The following program prints `20`.

```cpp
int a = 3, b = 7;
int result = a > b ? 10 : b > 5 ? 20 : 30;
cout << result;
```
